esearch/index.htm"/><comment>and <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.organdonationnow.org/"/></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Advisory Committee on Organ Transplantation</collab></person-group><article-title>Consensus Recommendations to the HHS Secretary </article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.organdonor.gov/research/acot.htm"/></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lynn</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Are the patients who become organ donors under the Pittsburgh protocol for "non-heart beating donors" really dead?</article-title><source>Kennedy Institute of Ethics Journal</source><year>1993</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>167</fpage><lpage>178</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10126529</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rady</surname><given-names>MY</given-names></name><name><surname>Verheijde</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name><name><surname>McGregor</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Organ donation after circulatory death: the forgotten donor</article-title><source>Critical Care</source><year>2006</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>166</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17020597</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/cc5038</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Whetstine</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An examination of the biophilosophical literature on the definition and criteria of death: when is dead dead and why some donation after cardiac death donors are not</article-title><source>Department of Health Care Ethics</source><year>2006</year><volume>PhD Dissertation</volume><publisher-name>Pittsburgh, PA , Duquesne University, Department of Health Care Ethics</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Crippen</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Whetstine</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ethics review: Dark angels - the problem of death in intensive care</article-title><source>Critical Care</source><year>2007</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>202</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17254317</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/cc5138</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>Committee on Non-Heart-Beating Transplantation II-The Scientific and Ethical Basis for Practice and Protocols-Division of Health Care Services-Institute of Medicine</collab></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Medicine I</surname></name></person-group><source>Non-Heart-Beating Organ Transplantation: Practice and Protocols</source><year>2000</year><edition>Edition 2000</edition><publisher-name>Washington, DC , National Academy Press</publisher-name><fpage>156</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>Ethics Committee of The American College of Critical Care Medicine and Society of Critical Care Medicine</collab></person-group><article-title>Recommendations for nonheartbeating organ donation: a position paper by the Ethics Committee, American College of Critical Care Medicine, Society of Critical Care Medicine</article-title><source>Critical Care Medicine</source><year>2001</year><volume>29</volume><fpage>1826</fpage><lpage>1831</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11546995</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/00003246-200109000-00029</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maleck</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Piper</surname><given-names>SN</given-names></name><name><surname>Triem</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Boldt</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Zittel</surname><given-names>FU</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Unexpected return of spontaneous circulation after cessation of resuscitation (Lazarus phenomenon)</article-title><source>Resuscitation</source><year>1998</year><volume>39</volume><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>128</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9918459</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0300-9572(98)00119-1</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Adhiyaman</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Sundaram</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Lazarus Phenomenon </article-title><source>Journal of The Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh</source><year>2002</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>9</fpage><lpage>13</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bartlett</surname><given-names>ET</given-names></name><name><surname>Bartlett</surname><given-names>ET</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Differences between death and dying</article-title><source>Journal of Medical Ethics</source><year>1995</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>270</fpage><lpage>276</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8558540</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>National Conference of Commissioners on Uniform State Laws</collab></person-group><article-title>National Conference of Commissioners on Uniform State Laws, The Unifor